      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Partial Fraction Decomposition With Higher Order Factors",
      "problem": "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{10-{x}^{2}}{(x-4)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{x-4}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(x-4)^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$, $B$ and $C$ are real constants, find the value of $A\\cdot B\\cdot C.$",
      "worked_solution": "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-4)^{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10-{x}^{2} & =A(x-4)^{2}+B(x-4)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=4$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10-{4}^{2} & =A(4-4)^{2}+B(4-4)+C \\\\ -6 & =A(0)+B(0)+C \\\\ C & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ and using our value for $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10-{0}^{2} & =A(0-4)^{2}+B(0-4)-6 \\\\ 10 & =16A-4B-6 \\\\ 0 & =16A-4B-16 \\\\ 0 & =4A-B-4 \\\\ B & =4A-4.\\,\\,(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, letting $x=3$ and using our value for $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10-{3}^{2} & =A(3-4)^{2}+B(3-4)-6 \\\\ 1 & =A-B-6 \\\\ 0 & =A-B-7 \\\\ B & =A-7.\\,\\,(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom $(1)$ and $(2)$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4A-4 & =A-7 \\\\ 3A & =-3 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the value of $A$ into $(2)$  to get the value of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =-1-7=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B\\cdot C & =-1\\cdot (-8)\\cdot (-6)=-48.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
